{"term":"catamite","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"pederasty","concept":"sex_slave","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"classical_specialized","crude":55,"technical":40,"recognition_pct_current":20,"recognition_pct_peak":30,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A boy kept by an older man for sexual purposes; French adoption of Latin catamitus, denoting a youth held as a pederastic sexual companion in Greco-Roman antiquity.","english_term":"catamite","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"From Latin catamitus, an Etruscan rendering of Greek Ganymede. Used in classically educated French literary contexts; nearest registry concept is sex_slave as catamites were typically enslaved or held in coercive dependence. Describes the victim of pederasty, not the perpetrator."}
{"term":"stupre","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"sexual_violence","concept":"ravishment","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":55,"technical":50,"recognition_pct_current":15,"recognition_pct_peak":35,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sexual defilement, violation, or ravishment; an archaic French legal and literary term for rape or licentious debauchery, derived from Latin stuprum.","english_term":"ravishment","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Broader than modern viol (rape) — covers both coerced violation and voluntary licentious debauchery in some uses. Survives mainly in historical legal texts and literary citations."}
{"term":"impudicité","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"licentiousness","concept":"licentiousness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":30,"technical":50,"recognition_pct_current":25,"recognition_pct_peak":55,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Immodesty, unchastity, or sexual shamelessness; the quality of being sexually or morally uninhibited without restraint, opposite of pudeur.","english_term":"unchastity","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"From Latin impudicitia. Used in Catholic moral theology and classical French literature to condemn sexual immorality. More formal and abstract than lasciveté."}
{"term":"turpitude charnelle","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"carnal_depravity","concept":"debauchery","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":35,"technical":45,"recognition_pct_current":20,"recognition_pct_peak":40,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Carnal depravity; a formal phrase for moral baseness or wickedness specifically in matters of the flesh and sexual conduct, used in religious and juridical contexts.","english_term":"carnal depravity","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Combines turpitude (moral baseness, disgrace) with charnelle (carnal/fleshly). Appears in Catholic moral theology and historical French penal law. The compound elevates condemnation to a formal legal-theological register."}
{"term":"péché de la chair","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"sexual_sin","concept":"carnal_desire","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":10,"technical":30,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sin of the flesh; the Catholic theological category encompassing all sexual sins committed against chastity, including fornication, adultery, masturbation, and lust.","english_term":"sin of the flesh","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Standard phrase in French Catholic moral theology. Covers mortal and venial sins of a sexual nature collectively. Contrasted with sins of the spirit. Familiar to virtually any French speaker with Catholic cultural background."}
{"term":"incontinent","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"sexual_restraint","concept":"licentiousness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":25,"technical":40,"recognition_pct_current":30,"recognition_pct_peak":55,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sexually unrestrained; lacking self-control over carnal urges in the archaic moral sense, distinct from the now-dominant modern medical meaning of urinary incontinence.","english_term":"incontinent","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Archaic sexual meaning derives from Latin incontinens (lacking self-restraint over desires). The modern medical sense (inability to control urination) has almost entirely displaced this usage in contemporary French."}
{"term":"sodomite","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"sodomy","concept":"sodomite","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":55,"technical":30,"recognition_pct_current":65,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A man who practices sodomy; a religious and legal-historical condemnation term for men who have anal sex, derived from the biblical city of Sodom, applied primarily against gay men.","english_term":"sodomite","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"French cognate of English sodomite with identical etymology and usage. Used in French Catholic theology and historical penal law; now archaic in everyday speech but still widely recognized. See also bougre for the French-specific vernacular cognate."}
{"term":"lasciveté","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"lasciviousness","concept":"lasciviousness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":40,"technical":45,"recognition_pct_current":25,"recognition_pct_peak":55,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Lasciviousness; the quality of being sexually wanton, lewd, or persistently given to lustful behavior.","english_term":"lasciviousness","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Archaic variant; the more common modern French form is lascivité. Both are rare in contemporary usage. Derived from Latin lascivia. Distinguished from the adjective lascif/lascive, which is more current."}
{"term":"priapée","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"phallic_poetry","concept":"priapus_deity","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"classical_specialized","crude":50,"technical":50,"recognition_pct_current":10,"recognition_pct_peak":20,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A priapic poem; a type of obscene Latin or French verse in the tradition of the Roman Priapea, celebrating the phallic deity Priapus; by extension, any licentious or phallic-themed literary work.","english_term":"priapic poem","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Derives from the Roman fertility god Priapus, always depicted with a permanent oversized erection. The Priapea is a collection of anonymous Latin epigrams in his honor. In French literary usage, priapée specifically denotes obscene phallic-themed verse."}
{"term":"grivoiserie","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"bawdy_humor","concept":"bawdy_humor","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":35,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":45,"recognition_pct_peak":65,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A bawdy joke, risqué story, or sexually suggestive remark; the quality of being cheerfully and playfully indecent without being overtly vulgar.","english_term":"bawdiness","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"From grivois (risqué, off-color). Implies lighthearted indecency rather than crude vulgarity — a staple of French literary and theatrical tradition. More refined in connotation than grossièreté."}
{"term":"gaudriole","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"bawdy_humor","concept":"bawdy_humor","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":40,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":30,"recognition_pct_peak":55,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A ribald joke, bawdy quip, or sexually suggestive witticism; an archaic synonym of grivoiserie implying merry, off-color humor.","english_term":"ribald joke","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Also used as a verb phrase: conter des gaudrioles (to tell off-color jokes). More archaic and less common than grivoiserie; survives mainly in literary and theatrical registers."}
{"term":"bougre","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"sodomy","concept":"sodomite","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":45,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Originally: a sodomite or heretic (from Bulgare, as Bogomil heretics from Bulgaria were accused of sodomy); now: a mild general insult meaning fellow, rascal, or bastard.","english_term":"bugger","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Cognate with English bugger, sharing identical etymology (Old French bougre from Medieval Latin Bulgarus) and the same semantic drift from sodomite to generic insult. The sexual meaning is almost entirely obsolete in modern French; bougrement functions as an intensifier meaning extremely."}
{"term":"foutriquet","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"insult","concept":"fucker_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":55,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":20,"recognition_pct_peak":45,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"An archaic French insult for a small, contemptible, sexually and socially insignificant man; a diminutive pejorative derived from foutre (to fuck) via the suffix -iquet.","english_term":"little shit","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The diminutive suffix -iquet intensifies contempt with implications of smallness and inadequacy. Etymology from foutre gives it a sexual edge beyond mere general insult. Now very archaic; rarely encountered outside historical or literary texts."}
{"term":"ribaud","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"libertine","concept":"libertine","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":50,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":20,"recognition_pct_peak":55,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A licentious man; a ruffian, debauchee, or sexually dissolute person in medieval and early modern French; often associated with soldiers, vagabonds, and camp followers.","english_term":"ribald","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The English adjective ribald derives from Old French ribaud. In medieval French also designated a class of mercenary soldiers and camp followers known for licentiousness. The feminine form is ribaude."}
